content:
Every client website we ship starts from the same place: a Next.js 16 + Tailwind CSS v4 boilerplate we have refined across dozens of projects. Today we are open-sourcing it. This post walks through **what is inside, why each decision was made, and how to use it** for your own production sites.

> If you just want the code, jump to the repository link at the end. If you want to understand *why* it is built this way, read on.

## Why a boilerplate at all?

Starting from `create-next-app` is great, but the first two days of every project were always the same:

- Setting up SEO metadata, Open Graph images and a sitemap
- Configuring fonts, colours and a design system in Tailwind
- Writing the same scroll-reveal animation for the fifth time
- Wiring up a contact form that actually sends email

Those two days add nothing unique to the client's product. The boilerplate turns them into ten minutes.

![The default home page of the boilerplate, ready to customise](/web-development.jpg)

## Folder structure

We use the App Router exclusively. Everything a route needs lives next to it, and shared UI lives in `app/components`.

```bash
app/
├── layout.jsx          # fonts, global metadata, providers
├── page.jsx            # home page
├── sitemap.js          # generated from routes + data
├── robots.js
├── components/         # shared UI (Header, Footer, Reveal...)
├── data/               # JSON content editable from the admin
└── api/
    └── send-email/     # contact form endpoint
```

The rule of thumb: **if only one page uses it, it lives in that page's folder.** Shared code earns its place in `components/` only once a second page needs it.

## SEO that works out of the box

Next.js has a first-class Metadata API, so there is no need for `next-seo` or hand-written `<head>` tags. The root layout defines the defaults and a title template:

```jsx
// app/layout.jsx
export const metadata = {
  metadataBase: new URL("https://example.com"),
  title: {
    default: "Acme Studio",
    template: "%s | Acme Studio",
  },
  description: "We design and build fast websites.",
  openGraph: { type: "website", siteName: "Acme Studio" },
};
```

Dynamic pages then override only what changes. Note that in Next.js 16 `params` is a **Promise**, so it must be awaited:

```jsx
// app/blog/[id]/page.jsx
export async function generateMetadata({ params }) {
  const { id } = await params;
  const post = await getPost(id);
  return {
    title: post.title,
    description: post.excerpt,
    openGraph: { type: "article", images: [post.image] },
  };
}
```

The sitemap is generated from the same data, so a new page is indexed without anyone remembering to update an XML file:

```js
// app/sitemap.js
export default async function sitemap() {
  const posts = await getPosts();
  return [
    { url: "https://example.com", lastModified: new Date() },
    ...posts.map((p) => ({
      url: `https://example.com/blog/${p.id}`,
      lastModified: p.date,
    })),
  ];
}
```

## Design tokens with Tailwind CSS v4

Tailwind v4 drops `tailwind.config.js` in favour of CSS-first configuration. Brand colours and fonts become CSS variables that every utility can use:

```css
/* app/globals.css */
@import "tailwindcss";

@theme {
  --color-brand-50: #eef2ff;
  --color-brand-600: #4f46e5;
  --font-display: "Inter", sans-serif;
}
```

Now `bg-brand-600` and `font-display` work everywhere, and rebranding a client site is a one-file change.

## Animations without the jank

Scroll reveals are the most copy-pasted code on the web. We ship one small component built on Framer Motion that only animates `opacity` and `transform`, the two properties the browser can animate on the GPU:

```jsx
"use client";
import { motion } from "framer-motion";

export default function Reveal({ children, delay = 0 }) {
  return (
    <motion.div
      initial={{ opacity: 0, y: 24 }}
      whileInView={{ opacity: 1, y: 0 }}
      viewport={{ once: true, margin: "-80px" }}
      transition={{ duration: 0.5, delay, ease: [0.22, 1, 0.36, 1] }}
    >
      {children}
    </motion.div>
  );
}
```

`viewport.once` matters: re-animating every time a section scrolls back into view looks playful in a demo and tiring on the third visit.

## Performance checklist

Every site built from the boilerplate is checked against this list before launch:

| Area | What we do | Why it matters |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Images | `next/image` with explicit sizes | Prevents layout shift (CLS) |
| Fonts | `next/font` or preconnected Google Fonts | No flash of invisible text |
| JavaScript | Server Components by default, `"use client"` only where needed | Smaller bundles, faster TTI |
| Data | Static or cached where possible | Pages served from the edge |
| Animation | `transform` and `opacity` only | Smooth 60fps scrolling |

On a typical marketing site this gets us **95+ Lighthouse scores** on mobile without any per-project tuning.

## See it in action

In this video we use the same setup to design a full e-commerce app and admin panel UI with Next.js and Tailwind CSS:

https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6dvYioHX328

## Getting started

```bash
git clone https://github.com/jay19patel/nextjs-boilerplate my-site
cd my-site
npm install
npm run dev
```

Then:

1. Update `metadata` in `app/layout.jsx` with your site name and URL
2. Set your brand colours in the `@theme` block of `globals.css`
3. Replace the content in `app/data/*.json`
4. Deploy to Vercel with one click

## What is next

We are working on an optional admin panel module (the one powering this very blog) and a set of pre-built sections: pricing tables, FAQs and testimonials. Star the repository to follow along, and if you build something with it, we would love to see it.
